Cryoglobulinemia
Rheumatology & Immunology
Palpable purpura, Polyarthralgias, Detectable serum cryoglobulins

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-010
Reason for Visit: Acute eruption of spreading, painful purpuric rash on the lower extremities, accompanied by severe polyarthralgia and progressive limb weakness, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude critical limb ischemia and acute renal compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a rapidly progressive, dark, non-blanching rash with severe joint pain and new-onset distal numbness following cold exposure. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was necessary to differentiate from acute meningococcemia, assess vascular integrity to prevent digital necrosis, and formulate a safe management plan, as waiting until the next daytime clinic posed a risk of irreversible ischemic or renal damage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are covered in dark purple bruises that hurt to touch, my knees and hands are aching terribly, and my toes feel completely dead."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 48-hour history of severe, bilateral polyarthralgias affecting the hands and knees, causing significant functional limitation. Twenty-four hours ago, following a period of moderate cold exposure, a recurrent palpable purpura predominantly concentrated on the lower extremities rapidly erupted. The patient describes profound, progressive weakness and fatigue. Notes that the skin on their thighs shows a net-like pattern (livedo reticularis) that is noticeably exacerbated by cold exposure. Furthermore, the patient complains of a new sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy, described as "pins and needles" progressing to numbness in the toes. Denies fevers or rigors. Known history of chronic Hepatitis C infection.
Functional Status: Housebound, mobilizing with extreme difficulty due to severe knee arthralgia and distal numbness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; impaired mobility and dexterity limit the ability to prepare food or self-care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 mmHg | HR: 95 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically fatigued, bundled in multiple blankets, and uncomfortable.
Physical Exam: Dermatological: Extensive, non-blanching palpable purpura distributed heavily over the bilateral shins and ankles. Marked livedo reticularis present on the thighs. Digits are cold to the touch with delayed capillary refill, consistent with Raynaud's phenomenon, though no frank digital necrosis is currently visible. Musculoskeletal: Bilateral symmetric tenderness and soft tissue swelling in the PIP joints of the hands and both knees; no massive effusions. Neurological: Decreased sensation to pinprick and light touch in a stocking distribution up to the mid-calf bilaterally. Motor strength slightly reduced distally. Abdomen: Mild hepatosplenomegaly palpable. Extremities: Mild pitting edema present at the ankles, raising suspicion for immune-complex glomerulonephritis. Integration of the diagnostic tetrad points heavily to clinical palpable purpura and polyarthralgias, driven by expected detectable serum cryoglobulins and low complement levels linked to the Hep C history.
Environmental Observations: House is kept quite cold (thermostat at 18 C); advised to immediately increase ambient temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with inadequate relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cryoglobulinemia - Status: Exacerbated (Vasculitic flare)
Differentials:

Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of systemic hemodynamic instability, bleeding from mucosal sites, and the clear provocation by cold exposure with localized palpable purpura rather than diffuse petechiae.

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The overwhelming presence of severe vasculitic skin manifestations (purpura, livedo) and prominent peripheral neuropathy, which are not typical of a primary RA flare.

Cold Agglutinin Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dominant clinical picture of severe polyarthralgias and palpable purpura, rather than symptoms purely driven by acute hemolytic anemia (like profound jaundice or hemoglobinuria).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, cold-provoked vasculitic flare featuring rapidly spreading palpable purpura, severe arthralgias, and new-onset peripheral neuropathy. In the context of known Hepatitis C, this strongly indicates a cryoglobulinemic crisis. Urgent after-hours assessment was vital to physically examine the digits for impending ischemic necrosis, assess for signs of acute glomerulonephritis (edema, hypertension), and implement strict thermal and medical management. Delaying this review carried a significant risk of irreversible peripheral nerve damage or digital gangrene, fully validating the use of the urgent MBS 585-600 item codes.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe high-dose NSAIDs (e.g., Naproxen) to manage the severe polyarthralgias, provided renal function is known to be stable. Urgent blood work (full blood count, renal panel, LFTs, complement levels, and cryoglobulins) and urinalysis (to check for protein/blood indicating glomerulonephritis) must be ordered via the regular GP immediately the next morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Strict thermal regulation: Set the home thermostat to exactly 23 degrees Celsius and wear thick woolen socks; observing the cessation of new purple spots and improved color in the toes will directly prove to the patient the physical necessity of avoiding cold exposure. Joint offloading: Use a supportive walking stick when mobilizing to the kitchen; the immediate reduction in knee pain will reinforce the use of the mobility aid during this acute flare.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Walking stick.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to organize laboratory investigations and consider referral for rheumatological/hepatological management of the underlying Hepatitis C and cryoglobulinemia.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the toes turn black or completely white and numb (signs of digital ischemia), if urine output drops significantly, or if severe abdominal pain develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The rash and joint pain are highly suspicious for proteins in your blood clumping together in the cold, related to your Hepatitis history. Specific blood tests and urine tests are required urgently tomorrow to check your kidneys.
Clinical Course: By strictly avoiding cold and managing the joint pain, the acute flare can stabilize. Long-term treatment usually requires managing the underlying viral infection and immune system.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep your body and your house very warm. Go straight to the hospital if any of your fingers or toes turn black or become completely numb, or if you stop passing urine, as these are medical emergencies.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Abnormal proteins in your blood are solidifying when exposed to the cold, blocking tiny blood vessels and causing the rash and nerve pain. Keeping strictly warm prevents this solidifying process while the anti-inflammatories calm the joints.
Actionable Steps: Turn your heater up immediately. Wear warm socks and gloves. See your regular doctor tomorrow morning without fail for kidney and blood tests.

